0351: cpu: no cache maintenance before WFI by default (kern.sweet_idle_cache=0); set/way maintenance bounded by CLIDR's levels

Needs 0344.

- kern.sweet_idle_cache / boot-arg sweet_idle_cache default 0: nothing before WFI. On these cores WFI keeps
  the core's state and its caches coherent; Linux idles them with dsb + wfi only (arch/arm64/kernel/
  idle.c), and this kernel never powers a core down (no PSCI CPU_SUSPEND or CPU_OFF); where firmware powers
  a core or cluster down, PSCI does the maintenance (TF-A psci_do_pwrdown_cache_maintenance). 1 (L1 and the
  cluster's L2, sweet_FlushL1L2_Dcache) and 2 (L1 only) remain for measurement.
- CleanPoC_Dcache, CleanPoU_Dcache, FlushPoC_Dcache and FlushPoU_Dcache turn CLIDR's count of levels into
  DCACHE_SET_WAY's last level (count - 1, nothing when 0), as Linux's set/way loops do: PoU is the L1 only,
  PoC the L1 and the L2 (the PoC paths did one extra, empty level; the PoU paths reached the L2).

Measured (home-screen workload, interleaved, n=2 per value, with 0344's switch): 0 against 1 gives 25-26
against 62-68 us of render-thread CPU per Metal draw and 44.0-46.9 against 29.6-31.6 command buffers/s.
Lines a CPU holds live longer with 0; 0392 and 0394 deal with two paths where that matters. Two SpringBoard
crashes on relaunch after a restart of backboardd (SIGBUS, SIGSEGV) were seen after running with 0 and none
the same day before it; their cause is not established.

